#!/bin/sh
set -e

# vpinfe's "slim" build doesn't bundle its own Chromium (the full build
# does -- 633M of its ~955M footprint, confirmed by inspecting the
# actual .deb contents). The slim build expects a system browser to
# already be present instead. No published checksum sidecar for
# Google's "current" direct-download URL (unlike vpinos-deb-repo's
# releases) -- relying on HTTPS transport + dpkg's own package
# integrity checks during unpack.

workdir=$(mktemp -d)
cd "$workdir"

curl -fsSL https://dl.google.com/linux/direct/google-chrome-stable_current_amd64.deb -o google-chrome-stable.deb
apt-get install -y ./google-chrome-stable.deb

cd /
rm -rf "$workdir"

# vpinfe invokes the plain "/usr/bin/google-chrome" name (an
# update-alternatives symlink) with no flags of its own, and Chrome
# defaults to the X11 Ozone platform -- confirmed directly: it fails
# immediately with "Missing X server or $DISPLAY" since there's no X11
# (or XWayland) here at all, only weston. Also running as root (which
# everything in this image's launch chain does) without --no-sandbox is
# a well-known Chrome failure on its own, on top of the Ozone issue --
# --no-sandbox is safe here specifically because Chrome only ever
# renders vpinfe's own local UI (served from 127.0.0.1), not arbitrary
# untrusted web content. Replacing the update-alternatives symlink with
# a wrapper is simplest: vpinfe keeps calling the same path, and gets
# the right flags transparently, without needing changes on vpinfe's
# own side.
#
# --use-gl=swiftshader --enable-unsafe-swiftshader: with the
# Ozone/sandbox issues fixed, Chrome ran with real, healthy-looking
# processes (confirmed via `ps`) and zero error output, but rendered
# pure black indefinitely -- not a crash, just never painting a frame.
# First tried --disable-gpu (the more commonly-suggested flag for
# GPU-less VMs), which didn't fix it either. The actual issue: Chrome's
# GPU process, even with hardware acceleration unavailable (VirtualBox's
# virtual GPU doesn't support real Vulkan/GL, already established), still
# needs an explicit, *working* GL backend to ever paint anything --
# `--disable-gpu` tries to eliminate the GPU process entirely, which is a
# different, likely counterproductive approach here. `--use-gl=swiftshader`
# keeps the GPU process but forces it onto Chrome's own software GL
# implementation (SwiftShader); `--enable-unsafe-swiftshader` is required
# alongside it on current Chrome, which gates SwiftShader behind explicit
# opt-in for security reasons. Worth revisiting once testing moves to
# real cabinet hardware with a real GPU, where forcing software
# rendering may cost performance for no reason.
# $real_chrome_wrapper (what /usr/bin/google-chrome resolves to,
# /opt/google/chrome/google-chrome) is ITSELF a bash wrapper script
# Google ships, not the compiled binary -- confirmed directly via
# strace on a real hang: a single execve() showed our own four flags
# repeated four times in one argv list. Chrome's multi-process
# architecture re-execs itself for every child process (renderer, GPU
# process, ...), and each hop through *two* layers of shell-script
# wrapping (ours, then Google's) re-added flags. Google's own wrapper
# also separately pipes stdout/stderr through `cat` subshells
# (`exec > >(exec cat)`), an extra buffering layer our own `stdbuf`
# fix in launch.sh never accounted for -- plausibly also why no client
# output ever reached the log during this whole debugging stretch.
# Fix: point straight at the real compiled binary
# ($HERE/chrome, alongside the wrapper -- confirmed directly by
# reading Google's wrapper script), skipping Google's wrapper layer
# entirely, not just avoiding our own double-wrapping.
real_chrome_wrapper=$(readlink -f /usr/bin/google-chrome)
real_chrome="$(dirname "$real_chrome_wrapper")/chrome"

wrapper_path=/opt/vpinball/google-chrome-wrapper.sh
mkdir -p /opt/vpinball
cat > "$wrapper_path" << WRAPPER
#!/bin/sh
exec "$real_chrome" --ozone-platform=wayland --no-sandbox --use-gl=swiftshader --enable-unsafe-swiftshader "\$@"
WRAPPER
chmod +x "$wrapper_path"

# Originally this directly overwrote /usr/bin/google-chrome's file
# content -- confirmed directly (via a real Calamares install test,
# not a guess) that this doesn't survive: update-alternatives' own
# state (/var/lib/dpkg/alternatives/google-chrome) never knew we'd
# bypassed it, so it still considers itself the owner of that path.
# Something in Calamares's post-install package cleanup (its
# `packages` module runs `apt-get remove`/`autoremove` on the target,
# which processes dpkg triggers system-wide as a side effect)
# re-synced /usr/bin/google-chrome back to a symlink pointing at
# Google's own postinst-registered choice (priority 200), silently
# undoing the fix -- confirmed via `ls -la /usr/bin/google-chrome` on
# a real installed system afterward. This never showed up in live-
# session testing, which boots straight from the squashfs with no
# such install-time dpkg activity. Registering our own wrapper as a
# HIGHER-priority alternative instead, rather than fighting
# update-alternatives by re-overwriting the raw file: its own default
# "auto" mode always selects the highest-priority registered choice,
# so ours keeps winning even after any future trigger reprocessing --
# working with the mechanism instead of bypassing it.
update-alternatives --install /usr/bin/google-chrome google-chrome "$wrapper_path" 300
